# Java Style

## Rules

- Organize imports in this order: (1) static, (2) `java`/`javax`, (3) third-party, (4) project. No wildcard imports.
- Declare every member with the most restrictive visibility that satisfies its usage; default to `private`.
- Extract repeated string literals into `private static final String` constants in `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`.
- Order methods: constructors → public → package-private → private helpers.
- Annotate every overriding method with `@Override`.
- Add one blank line before `return` when the method body has at least one preceding statement; omit it for a single-`return` body.
- Use `var` only when the inferred type is unambiguous from the right-hand side.
- Use the ternary operator only for single-expression assignments; never nest ternaries.
- Extract multi-statement logic and complex conditions into private helpers named by intent (`getSupportedLocales()`, not `buildLocaleList()`). Avoid generic prefixes `build`, `compute`, or `process` when a domain name exists.
- Do not extract a helper that is called only once and is trivially readable inline.

```java
class HolidayService {
  private static final String MISSING_ID = "holiday.id.missing";

  private final HolidayRepository holidayRepository;

  HolidayService(HolidayRepository holidayRepository) {
    this.holidayRepository = Objects.requireNonNull(holidayRepository, "holidayRepository");
  }

  Optional<HolidayResponse> findById(long id) {
    Optional<HolidayModel> holiday = holidayRepository.findById(id);

    return holiday.map(this::toResponse);
  }

  private HolidayResponse toResponse(HolidayModel holiday) {
    return new HolidayResponse(holiday.id(), holiday.name());
  }
}
```
